[00:01.76]The lucky draw spins slow beneath the cold mall light,
[00:09.71]Teresa holds her number like a prayer.
[00:16.32]She's watched the prizes all night long, the blind boxes stacked tight,
[00:25.59]and let herself believe one's waiting there.
[00:34.41]The girl ahead tears open a little vinyl king,
[00:41.91]the boy beside her gasps — a rare, the gold.
[00:49.41]And everyone is cradling some small, beloved thing,
[00:56.91]while Teresa's hands stay empty in the cold.
[01:05.74]'Cause it's two dollars of sunshine,
[01:12.35]two dollars of gold,
[01:17.21]the same paper voucher
[01:22.50]each time the wheel slows.
[01:27.35]Not the box that she dreamed of,
[01:33.53]not the prize on the shelf —
[01:38.82]just a sweet cup of orange
[01:45.00]she'll drink by herself.
[01:51.18]She walks up to the corner where the oranges still spin,
[02:00.44]and feeds the little voucher through the seam.
[02:07.94]They tumble and they're halved with that soft, familiar whir —
[02:17.21]the only thing tonight that came for her.
[02:25.15]And maybe it's the sweetest cup around,
[02:31.76]and maybe that's the saddest part she's found:
[02:38.82]she'd trade away the orange and the gold
[02:45.88]for one small box that's hers to have and hold.
[02:55.15]So it's two dollars of sunshine,
[03:01.76]two dollars of gold,
[03:06.62]and the line shuffles on
[03:11.91]and the evening grows old.
[03:16.76]She lifts up the cup,
[03:21.62]takes a sip, gives a smile —
[03:26.91]sweet Teresa and Ijooz,
[03:31.76]for a little while.
